<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guy of Lusignan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1150–<a href="/years/1194">1194</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as King Guy · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lusignan">House of Lusignan</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guy of Lusignan was a French nobleman who became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> through his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibylla-queen-of-jerusalem">Queen Sibylla</a>. He commanded the crusader army that was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187, where he was captured. The defeat allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> to take Jerusalem and most of the crusader kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan.jpg" alt="Guy of Lusignan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Museum of the The Order of St. John and The University of Birmingham · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_issued_by_Guy_de_Lusignan_during_when_he_was_Lord_of_Cyprus_(Crop).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Guy was born around 1150, the youngest son of Hugh VIII of Lusignan, in Poitou, which belonged to the territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>. His family had long taken part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>, and his father died in Muslim captivity in the 1160s after the Battle of Harim. On 27 March 1168 Guy and his brothers ambushed and killed Patrick, 1st Earl of Salisbury, who was escorting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, and the brothers were banished from Poitou. Guy went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> between 1173 and 1180. In 1180, to forestall a coup by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond III of Tripoli</a> and Bohemond III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, he was hastily married to Sibylla, the sister of Baldwin IV, whose leprosy made the succession uncertain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In June 1183 Baldwin made Guy regent, but the leading nobles refused to cooperate with him, and Baldwin, who had recovered, judged him incompetent, dismissed him and had Sibylla's five-year-old son crowned as Baldwin V. Baldwin IV died in 1185 and Baldwin V in 1186. Sibylla was crowned queen after agreeing to annul her marriage, on condition that she could choose her next husband, and she then chose Guy and crowned him. Raymond of Tripoli refused him homage until after the Battle of Cresson on 1 May 1187.</p><p>On 2 July 1187 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a>. Raymond advised against battle, but Gerard of Ridefort and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a> accused him of cowardice, and Guy marched against Saladin. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> the crusader army was annihilated and most of its commanders, Guy among them, were captured. Saladin beheaded Raynald and spared Guy, and in the following months the whole kingdom fell to Saladin except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. Guy was released in 1188.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a>, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, refused Guy entry, so Guy began the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1189. Sibylla and her daughters died in an epidemic in 1190, and Conrad married her half-sister Isabella and claimed the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> backed Conrad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> backed Guy. After Acre fell on 11 July 1191, the nobility chose Conrad at an assembly on 16 April 1192. Richard gave Guy Cyprus, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a> killed Conrad on 28 April 1192.</p><p>Guy ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> until his death around the end of 1194, and he proved a competent ruler there. With no surviving heir, he left Cyprus to his brother Geoffrey, but his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> elected his brother Aimery. Guy was highly unpopular among the nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and is often blamed for the fall of the kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guy of Lusignan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The victory was total: the army was destroyed, the relic of the True Cross captured and <b>King Guy</b> of Lusignan taken prisoner.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Hattin on 4 July 1187, Saladin surrounded and destroyed the army of <b>King Guy</b> of Lusignan, perhaps 20,000 men, cut off from water near Lake Tiberias.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Guy of Lusignan</b>, King of Jerusalem, began the siege of Acre, his first significant counterattack against Saladin and part of what became the Third Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-28">28 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Tiberias in Galilee, Sultan Saladin destroyed the army of the Crusader Kingdom of Jerusalem and captured its king, <b>Guy of Lusignan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-4">4 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The county was frequently held by members of the royal family, including Amalric and later <b>Guy of Lusignan</b>, and its revenues came from the port and coastal farmland.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-jaffa-and-ascalon">County of Jaffa and Ascalon</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1192 Richard I sold Cyprus, recently taken from Isaac Komnenos and returned by the Templars, to <b>Guy of Lusignan</b>, the deposed king of Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cyprus">Kingdom of Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Poitou, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.65,-0.25&amp;year=1178" aria-label="Poitou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4544 524 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guy of Lusignan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4994 794h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4994 794h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.65,-0.25&amp;year=1178">What was here in 1178? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
